>Soldier ants can appear by Minetown for an XL1 character 
>unless Minetown is generated very, very high in the dungeon,

Let's see. Here's a quote from the steelypips monster generation FAQ:

--------
DIF is the monster's difficulty value. Monsters will start being
eligible for random generation when the average of your experience level
and your dungeon level is equal to DIF; they will be considered too weak
for generation when your dungeon level is 7*DIF or greater. (For this
purpose, once you have the Amulet of Yendor your dungeon level is
considered to be that of the Sanctum, and in the endgame to be the depth
of the Sanctum plus half your experience level.) The level of your god's
appreciation of a sacrificed corpse is also dependent on the corpse's
DIF.
--------

Soldier ants are DIF 6. So the sum of DL+XL must be 12. So at XL1 the DL
must be at least DL11. Minetown is never that deep in the dungeon.

In addition a good racketeer has buffed the pet so that by the time ants
do appear, the pet is big and fast enough to tackle it. Finally a good
racketeer has gotten the divine AC by the time they encounter ants and
has used that AC flexibility to either make themselves harder to hit,
faster (not burdened), a more consistent spellcaster, or a combination
of the three.


>and they are still 
>the #1 killer of early-game characters by a long shot.

Very true. But I've never encountered them in my initial forays into the
main dungeon while cleaning out shops, or in my trips down to Minetown. 

When I used to level up normally, they'd routinely show up about DL6. I
found I was ill equipped to deal with them. After a successful racket
run, they are no more fearsome than anything else in that same ballpark.

>Also, there is no "jump" in opponent difficulty with an increase in DL.  At
>level 6 or so, the level 0 monsters (goblin, kobold, etc.) fall off the
>difficulty map,

Actaully the level 0 monsters are DIF 1. According to the rules, they
hang around until DL 7*DIF. Even for small values of DIF (2-3) that'll
easily mean they can be generated up to and beyond the quest.

>but the upper range of monsters has already grown significantly
>by that point.  It's a slowly graded increase of which XL is only a part.

Half. XL can have a siginifcant impact on the monsters that you come
across. A XL8 character will see a significantly different set of
monsters at DL6 than an XL1 character.

>A more reliable and safer strategy is simply to go ahead and boost your
>XL as fast as possible so that you get the extra HP, skill slots, and to-hit
>bonus; this will work out much better for you in general, especially where the
>hit bonus is concerned (since it will be easier to kill monsters who are
>trying to eat your face.)

That's the prevailing wisdom. And that wisdom is always backed by the
fact that serial ascenders use this strategy, so it must be the right
one. But the counter argument is the fact that soldier ants are the
number 1 killer of nethack characters on NAO. And that's a completely
preventable encounter.

To paraphrase Bill Clinton "It's the AC stupid!". All the items you list
above are not helpful if you're getting whacked on at every turn.

You and I both disagree with xanthian about keeping XL depressed
throughout the game. I'm not a pacifist. In the long run it's a loser 
because eventually the DL will far overwhelm the XL in the monster
generation algorithm anyway. In addition you have to get to XL14 at some
point in the game to ascend.

But in the early game managing XL can be a successful strategy because
it does make the road easier while trying to attain the needed AC to
contend with hazards down the road. And while there are clearly other
roads to getting AC (I love dwarven mithril for example) they each have
impacts on other aspects of game play (weight, spellcasting effect) that
need to be accounted for. The Divine AC boost gives AC help in the early
game without impacting those other factors.

Also I'm not slavish about keeping XL down. If during a run I need to
get to XL3 or XL4 to be successful, I'll do that. Xanthian tends to lock
in on XL1 because his goal is to get to naked AC -10. While that's fine
for him, I personally find that goal a bit obsessive. I want AC
flexibility, not AC devotion. As the game progresses, enchantments and
praying divine AC makes it pretty much moot anyway.

So it's a early game strategy for me. An alternative, not just the
perfect path.

Finally see my other posts from the past on HP/PW boosts during the run
including judicious levelling up.

